Update ELK Installation and configuring Authentication/TLS on Elastic Server:

--------------------------------------------
Update /etc/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.yml

cluster.name: elasticsrch-cluster
node.name: node-1 #(it does not necessarily have to match the hostname)
path.data: /var/lib/elasticsearch
path.logs: /var/log/elasticsearch

network.host: 127.0.0.1 (default address) #(for example, in case of LAN, set to the server's private IP@ = 10.0.0.11, or to 0.0.0.0 = listen on all available network interfaces)
http.port: 9200

discovery.type: single-node #(in case of 1 node in the cluster)

xpack.security.enabled: true #(to enable security features: auth, TLS enforcement, RBAC "Role-Based Access Control")
xpack.security.http.ssl.enabled: true #(to encrypt the traffic between the clients (logstash-kibana-curl) and Elasticsearch, it means Elasticsearch's REST API uses HTTPS instead of plain HTTP, it will be https://SERVER-IP@:9200) 
xpack.security.transport.ssl.enabled: true #(to encrypt the elasticsearch node-to-node communication)
xpack.security.transport.ssl.verification_mode: certificate

#certificate: recommended by Elastic -- an appropriate verification mode for transport if the cluster topology changes often, because transport certs are issued per-CA-trust rather than matching hostname/IP for every node "full" (nodes get added/removed, so IPs change often), unless we have a stable DNS/hostnames per node.

************************************************************
In case of multiple nodes, for load balancing and redundancy purposes (for example, 3 nodes 10.0.0.11, 10.0.0.12, 10.0.0.13 // subnet mask 255.255.255.0 -- The decimal equivalent /24):

We do not need discovery.type, instead the nodes discover each other using discovery.seed_hosts:

cluster.name: elasticsrch-cluster
node.name: node-1 #(change it to node-2 / node-3 on other servers)

network.host: 0.0.0.0 (a routable IP@ 0.0.0.0)
http.port: 9200
transport.port: 9300 #(default port, for communication between nodes)

#add the firewall rules to allow only the authorized subnet, since the ports are listening on all interfaces:
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="10.0.0.0/24" port port="9200" protocol="tcp" accept'
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="10.0.0.0/24" port port="9300" protocol="tcp" accept'
sudo firewal-cmd --reload

cluster.initial_master_nodes: #(bootstrap --start-- the cluster using an initial set of master-eligible nodes, it means which nodes participate in creating the new cluster)
- node-1
- node-2
- node-3

#!Important: after the cluster successfully formed for the first time, remove cluster.initial_master_nodes from the configuration. This setting is only meant for the very first bootstrap. For the regular restarts, rolling updates or adding new nodes, leave it removed. With this misconfiguration, it may cause a Split-brain at the cluster management level. Once this occurs, data loss happens in two ways:: Divergent Data: some clients will write data to "Cluster A" and others to "Cluster B" // Overwritten Cluster State: bootstrapping a new cluster and pointing to an existing data path, it can overwrite the cluster metadata on disk. The original indices and data mapping become invisible to Elasticsearch, rendering the existing data unrecoverable.

discovery.seed_hosts: #(a string containing the addresses separated by commas, the default value for discovery.seed_hosts is ["127.0.0.1", "[::1]"])
- 10.0.0.11
- 10.0.0.12
- 10.0.0.13

*********************************************
Set Built-in Users Password:
cd /usr/share/elasticsearch/bin
./elasticsearch-reset-password -u elastic -i

#for the user= elastic
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
